<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=marine-chronometer">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marine chronometer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1761">1761</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A marine chronometer is a precision timepiece carried on a ship to find its longitude by comparing Greenwich Mean Time with local time, first developed in the 18th century: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> carpenter John Harrison's H4 of 1761 solved the precision problems of keeping time at sea. Harrison submitted it for the £20,000 longitude prize that the British government had offered in 1714. Pierre Le Roy's detent escapement and the simplified escapements of Thomas Earnshaw and John Arnold shaped later instruments, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> was supplying routinely to its ships by 1825.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marine-chronometer.jpg" alt="Marine chronometer" width="250" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phantom Photographer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H1_low_250.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Until the mid-1750s, accurate navigation at sea out of sight of land was an unsolved problem because longitude was hard to calculate. Navigators could find latitude from the angle of the sun at noon or of Polaris, but longitude required a time standard that would work aboard a ship. Gemma Frisius first proposed using a chronometer for the purpose in 1530. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pendulum-clock">Pendulum clocks</a> were useless at sea, since the rolling of a ship and variations in gravity of up to 0.2% disturbed them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-huygens">Christiaan Huygens</a> made the first attempt at a marine chronometer in 1673 and in 1675 devised one regulated by a balance wheel and spiral spring, but it remained imprecise at sea.</p><p>In 1714 the British government offered a longitude prize, with awards from £10,000 to £20,000 depending on accuracy. John Harrison, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> carpenter, completed a clock in 1735 with counter-oscillating weighted beams, and his H1 and H2 used that system. H3 of 1759 introduced the bi-metallic strip and caged roller bearings but was still inaccurate. His much smaller H4 solved the precision problems in 1761, with a fast-beating balance wheel and a temperature-compensated spiral spring, and he submitted it for the £20,000 prize.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The chronometer keeps the time of a known place, usually Greenwich. The difference between it and local time found from celestial observations gives the ship's longitude, and every four seconds of error can put the east–west position off by just over one nautical mile. It was kept below decks in a box in gimbals, and a hack watch set from it was carried on deck for observations. Ships often checked chronometers against the time ball at the Royal Observatory, Greenwich, before a voyage.</p><p>The Royal Observatory began testing chronometers in 1820 in an Admiralty trial, and observatories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> later certified them in chronometer competitions. Accuracy of mechanical marine chronometers reached about 0.5 second per day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Pierre Le Roy invented the detent escapement in 1748, and in 1766 produced a chronometer that Rupert Gould considered the foundation of the modern one. Thomas Earnshaw and John Arnold developed simplified spring detent escapements from 1780, which served as the basis of marine chronometers until the electronic era. The instruments were at first so expensive that not all ships carried them, as the wreck of the East Indiaman Arniston with the loss of 372 lives showed, but by 1825 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> was supplying its vessels routinely.</p><p>It is argued that without them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>'s ascendancy might not have occurred so overwhelmingly. Mechanical chronometers were mass-produced in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, notably by Hamilton in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marine chronometer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1761</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>John Harrison · <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Christiaan Huygens built a balance-spring chronometer in 1675 that kept poor time at sea, and some see Pierre Le Roy’s of 1766 as the basis of the modern chronometer.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/mechanical-clock">Mechanical clock<span>by 1300</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=marine-chronometer">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
